en":[{"tag":"span","props":{"className":"life-glossary-number"},"children":["02"]},{"tag":"h2","props":{},"children":["HR"]},{"tag":"p","props":{},"children":["Hazard ratio. The speed at which two groups hit an event (death, illness) over the same time. HR 0.87 means 13% lower than the control group; HR 1.21 means 21% higher"]}]},{"tag":"article","props":{"className":"life-glossary-row","id":"term-03","lang":"en"},"children":[{"tag":"span","props":{"className":"life-glossary-number"},"children":["03"]},{"tag":"h2","props":{},"children":["RR"]},{"tag":"p","props":{},"children":["Relative risk. The ratio of event probabilities between two groups; read like HR"]}]},{"tag":"article","props":{"className":"life-glossary-row","id":"term-04","lang":"en"},"children":[{"tag":"span","props":{"className":"life-glossary-number"},"children":["04"]},{"tag":"h2","props":{},"children":["OR"]},{"tag":"p","props":{},"children":["Odds ratio. The ratio of odds between two groups; close to RR when events are rare, exaggerates the difference when they are common"]}]},{"tag":"article","props":{"className":"life-glossary-row","id":"term-05","lang":"en"},"children":[{"tag":"span","props":{"className":"life-glossary-number"},"children":["05"]},{"tag":"h2","props":{},"children":["IRR"]},{"tag":"p","props":{},"children":["Incidence rate ratio; read like RR"]}]},{"tag":"article","props":{"className":"life-glossary-row","id":"term-06","lang":"en"},"children":[{"tag":"span","props":{"className":"life-glossary-number"},"children":["06"]},{"tag":"h2","props":{},"children":["RaR"]},{"tag":"p","props":{},"children":["Rate ratio (e.g. number of falls); read like RR"]}]},{"tag":"article","props":{"className":"life-glossary-row","id":"term-07","lang":"en"},"children":[{"tag":"span","props":{"className":"life-glossary-number"},"children":["07"]},{"tag":"h2","props":{},"children":["Standardized mortality ratio"]},{"tag":"p","props":{},"children":["SMR. Actual deaths in a group divided by the expected number computed from the mortality of the general population of the same age. 5.86 means 5.86 times the deaths of peers"]}]},{"tag":"article","props":{"className":"life-glossary-row","id":"term-08","lang":"en"},"children":[{"tag":"span","props":{"className":"life-glossary-number"},"children":["08"]},{"tag":"h2","props":{},"children":["Risk difference"]},{"tag":"p","props":{},"children":["The probability of an event in one group minus the other, directly giving \"how many extra cases per thousand\". Ratios only say how many times; the risk difference says how many more people in absolute terms"]}]},{"tag":"article","props":{"className":"life-glossary-row","id":"term-09","lang":"en"},"children":[{"tag":"span","props":{"className":"life-glossary-number"},"children":["09"]},{"tag":"h2","props":{},"children":["95% CI"]},{"tag":"p","props":{},"children":["95% confidence interval. The range where the true value most likely sits. If a ratio-type interval crosses 1, or a difference-type interval crosses 0, the difference may be pure chance and the text will say \"not statistically significant\""]}]},{"tag":"article","props":{"className":"life-glossary-row","id":"term-10","lang":"en"},"children":[{"tag":"span","props":{"className":"life-glossary-number"},"children":["10"]},{"tag":"h2","props":{},"children":["RCT"]},{"tag":"p","props":{},"children":["Randomized controlled trial. People are randomly split into two groups, one gets the intervention, one does not, and outcomes are compared. The best evidence of causation"]}]},{"tag":"article","props":{"className":"life-glossary-row","id":"term-11","lang":"en"},"children":[{"tag":"span","props":{"className":"life-glossary-number"},"children":["11"]},{"tag":"h2","props":{},"children":["Meta-analysis"]},{"tag":"p","props":{},"children":["Pooling the results of multiple studies into one overall estimate"]}]},{"tag":"article","props":{"className":"life-glossary-row","id":"term-12","lang":"en"},"children":[{"tag":"span","props":{"className":"life-glossary-number"},"children":["12"]},{"tag":"h2","props":{},"children":["Cohort"]},{"tag":"p","props":{},"children":["Cohort study. A group of people is followed for years to see who has events. Shows association, cannot fully show causation"]}]},{"tag":"article","props":{"className":"life-glossary-row","id":"term-13","lang":"en"},"children":[{"tag":"span","props":{"className":"life-glossary-number"},"children":["13"]},{"tag":"h2","props":{},"children":["Observational"]},{"tag":"p","props":{},"children":["Observational study. Researchers only observe, do not intervene; results may be affected by confounding and reverse causation, so discount the numbers"]}]},{"tag":"article","props":{"className":"life-glossary-row","id":"term-14","lang":"en"},"children":[{"tag":"span","props":{"className":"life-glossary-number"},"children":["14"]},{"tag":"h2","props":{},"children":["Confounding"]},{"tag":"p","props":{},"children":["A third factor that affects both cause and result, making association look like causation. People who eat more vegetables also tend to exercise more"]}]},{"tag":"article","props":{"className":"life-glossary-row","id":"term-15","lang":"en"},"children":[{"tag":"span","props":{"className":"life-glossary-number"},"children":["15"]},{"tag":"h2","props":{},"children":["Reverse causation"]},{"tag":"p","props":{},"children":["Not A causing B, but B causing A. It is not that sleeping a lot makes people die early; it is that seriously ill people sleep a lot"]}]},{"tag":"article","props":{"className":"life-glossary-row","id":"term-16","lang":"en"},"children":[{"tag":"span","props":{"className":"life-glossary-number"},"children":["16"]},{"tag":"h2","props":{},"children":["d, g"]},{"tag":"p","props":{},"children":["Effect size. How many standard deviations apart two group means are. 0.2 is small, 0.5 medium, 0.8 large"]}]},{"tag":"article","props":{"className":"life-glossary-row","id":"term-17","lang":"en"},"children":[{"tag":"span","props":{"className":"life-glossary-number"},"children":["17"]},{"tag":"h2","props":{},"children":["r"]},{"tag":"p","props":{},"children":["Correlation coefficient. How strongly two things move together, from -1 to 1; 0.1 weak, 0.3 medium, 0.5 strong"]}]},{"tag":"article","props":{"className":"life-glossary-row","id":"term-18","lang":"en"},"children":[{"tag":"span","props":{"className":"life-glossary-number"},"children":["18"]},{"tag":"h2","props":{},"children":["MET"]},{"tag":"p","props":{},"children":["Unit of exercise intensity. 1 MET is sitting quietly; brisk walking is about 3 to 4 MET. MET·h is intensity times hours"]}]},{"tag":"article","props":{"className":"life-glossary-row","id":"term-19","lang":"en"},"children":[{"tag":"span","props":{"className":"life-glossary-number"},"children":["19"]},{"tag":"h2","props":{},"children":["GRADE"]},{"tag":"p","props":{},"children":["An international standard for grading evidence quality into high, moderate, low, and very low"]}]},{"tag":"article","props":{"className":"life-glossary-row","id":"term-20","lang":"en"},"children":[{"tag":"span","props":{"className":"life-glossary-number"},"children":["20"]},{"tag":"h2","props":{},"children":["Intention-to-screen analysis"]},{"tag":"p","props":{},"children":["Counting the effect by \"who was invited\" rather than \"who actually went\", which underestimates the effect on those who actually went"]}]},{"tag":"article","props":{"className":"life-glossary-row","id":"term-21","lang":"en"},"children":[{"tag":"span","props":{"className":"life-glossary-number"},"children":["21"]},{"tag":"h2","props":{},"children":["Pack-years"]},{"tag":"p","props":{},"children":["A unit of smoking amount. Packs per day times years of smoking; 30 pack-years is one pack a day for 30 years"]}]},{"tag":"article","props":{"className":"life-glossary-row","id":"term-22","lang":"en"},"children":[{"tag":"span","props":{"className":"life-glossary-number"},"children":["22"]},{"tag":"h2","props":{},"children":["BMI"]},{"tag":"p","props":{},"